## Auth Middleware Pattern

```ts
// middlewares/auth.middleware.ts
export async function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.replace('Bearer ', '')
  if (!token) return res.status(401).json({ status: 'error', message: 'Unauthorized' })

  const payload = verifyAccessToken(token)  // throws on invalid
  req.user = { id: payload.sub, role: payload.role }
  next()
}

export function requireRole(role: UserRole) {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    if (req.user.role !== role) {
      return res.status(403).json({ status: 'error', message: 'Forbidden' })
    }
    next()
  }
}

// Usage in routes:
router.post('/workers/profile', requireAuth, requireRole('worker'), createProfile)
```

---

## General Rules

- Every async function must have try/catch or use an `asyncHandler` wrapper
- Never use `any` — use `unknown` and narrow it
- Never put `console.log` in production code — use a logger (pino recommended)
- Every DB query goes through a repository — never write raw DB calls in controllers or services
- All env vars go in `config/env.ts` with Zod validation on startup
- Keep functions under 30 lines — if longer, extract a helper
- Name booleans with `is_` / `has_` / `can_` prefix: `is_verified`, `has_payout`, `can_apply`
- Dates always stored as UTC, returned as ISO 8601 strings
- UUIDs generated by DB (`gen_random_uuid()`), not the application layer
